<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odyssey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Odyssey is an ancient Greek epic poem attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, probably composed in the late 8th or 7th century BCE. Its 24 books tell of the ten-year journey home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, and of his revenge on the suitors of his wife Penelope. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> it is one of the founding works of Western literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odyssey.jpg" alt="Odyssey" width="320" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragment_Odyssee_2245_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>There is no consensus on when the Odyssey and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> were composed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> began adopting a modified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> in the 8th century BCE, and if the Homeric poems were among its earliest products they would have been composed towards the end of that century. Scholars agree that the epics developed in an oral tradition over hundreds of years. In the early twentieth century Milman Parry and Albert Lord showed that they have the marks of oral poetry, which lets even an illiterate poet improvise large poems. The poem was performed for audiences by a singer called an aoidos or rhapsode, and by the mid-6th century BCE it was part of the Greek literary canon.</p><p>In antiquity <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s authorship was taken as true, though most scholars now assume that the two epics were composed independently, and the Homeric Question about their authorship still divides them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem has 24 books and 12,109 lines of dactylic hexameter. It opens in medias res, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, still absent ten years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. His journey home lasts another ten years, and all his crewmates are killed. At home 108 suitors court his wife Penelope, who says she will remarry when she has finished weaving a shroud for Odysseus's aged father Laertes, and each night secretly unweaves it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> sends his son Telemachus to seek news of his father, and he visits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>; this part, Books 1 to 4, is called the Telemachy.</p><p>Homecoming, nostos, is the central theme, with wandering, guest-friendship, testing and omens. The Phaeacians show exemplary guest-friendship, while Polyphemus and Calypso show poor guest-friendship. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> turns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> into a beggar so that he can test who is loyal. Penelope tests him in turn by saying she will move their bed, which was made from a living tree, a fact that only the real Odysseus would know.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> the epics remained school texts through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Livius Andronicus made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version, the Odusia, which became a school text for Latin students. The first printed edition in Greek appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1488, and the first complete English translation in 1616. The epics formed the basis of education in the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Western humanists adopted that curriculum. A poll of experts by BBC Culture in 2018 named it literature's most enduring narrative.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odyssey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek memory linked the city to its Bronze Age past: the Iliad names Knossos first among the Cretan cities led by Idomeneus, and the <b>Odyssey</b> calls it the great city where Minos ruled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the <b>Odyssey</b> into Latin around 240 BCE, Plautus and Terence adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic Annales, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, Catullus, Sallust and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iliad and the <b>Odyssey</b> of Homer, composed around the 8th or 7th century BCE, show the gods taking part in the Trojan War and the wanderings of Odysseus, intervening in human affairs, favouring their heroes and quarrelling on Olympus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek literature begins with two of its greatest works, the Iliad and the <b>Odyssey</b>, attributed to Homer and composed around 700 BCE from a long tradition of oral poetry.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Livius Andronicus, a Greek from southern Italy, translated the <b>Odyssey</b> and staged the first Latin play adapted from a Greek original in 240 BCE; Plautus and Terence wrote comedies, and Ennius wrote an epic history of Rome in Greek metre.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer’s <b>Odyssey</b> calls Crete a land of ninety cities and names Knossos as the city where Minos ruled.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
